Z||000002||fierospeeder||11-28-2001||03:44 PM||||go to your newstand and look for a vw trends magazine or a  hot vws and dune buggies.<P><BR>you can buy a complete stock trans for 200 bucks, beats a fiero.   If you want a trans for more peformance they go up in price.  The most expensive is around 1000.<P>I cant remember if there is a mount for a v8 chevy.  I do know there is conversions for a corvair engine and a ford 2.3l engine.<P>There is a custom chassis you can buy and put a v8 engine on it with a rear axle and put the bug body on top.  I have to look for this article.  <P>I got 30 years of vw magazines and theres 2 different magazines.  Ill try to scan couple of mags and see if i can find anything.<P>But your looking at the cheapest 150? for a rebuilt trans.  Im not sure if they are still selling it for that cheap still.<P>Unless a trip the junkyard will do it.<P><BR>heres also a newsgroup im in that you can ask these guys if they know of a v8 adapter and a trans for sale<BR> <A HREF="http://groups.google.com/groups?hl=en&safe=off&group=rec.autos.makers.vw.aircooled" TARGET=_blank>http://groups.google.com/groups?hl=en&safe=off&group=rec.autos.makers.vw.aircooled</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>Fiero Technician<P>74 Vw Beetle<BR>83 Honda Nighthawk 550<BR>84 Pontiac Fiero<BR>87 Ford Thunderbird Turbo<P>Selling a car is like giving your own kid for adoption.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||11-28-2001||09:33 PM||||You mean an off-road buggy? Forget the 350...keep it simple and light. My friends and I built one a few years ago. We beat the piss out of it and never had any major mechanical failures. We did blow appart a CV joint after coming down from about six feet of air. You should be able to buy a complete Beetle for $200-300. Here are some pics of mine. We called it the mud-bug.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buggy.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000004||KRMFiero||11-28-2001||09:37 PM||||haha nice.. trying to find VW engines around here are hard....    im having enough trouble looking for a trans axle<BR>Kyle, ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||11-29-2001||12:55 AM||||If you want to build a dune buggy, you want a VW bus (type 2) transaxle.  They're much stronger and use better, larger CV joints than the bug (type 1).  Specifically the 75-79 units are desired, as they already have fourth gear welded/brazed to the spline..under high torque it will shear, usually first thing to go in a VW tranny, but only if you're putting 200+ horse to it and are abusing it.  The late VW bus transaxles are referred to as a "five rib" transmission because they have five ribs around the main gear housing section, look very robust.  There is really very little difference in mounting a bus or bug trans, and adapters are very very cheap, so there's not point in going with a type 1 transmission.<P>You can find them all over...most junkyards won't have an old VW, but if you look for a VW junkyard I guarantee they'll have a TON!  You may have to drive a couple hours, but even then it's still worth it to know you got it from somebody who knows what the hell they're talking about.<P>As far as adapters, yes, you can get an adapter for ANY engine to a VW trans.  Also remember that the old porsche transaxles share the same bolt pattern (some even use the same clutch/flywheel, but aren't much stronger).  The 901 and 915 porsche trannies are as strong as a mildly built bus transaxle stock, but are a little harder to come by and cost more to work on.  The best place for adapters (the authority) is <A HREF="http://www.kennedyeng.com" TARGET=_blank>Kennedy Engineered Products</A>.  I have their adapter for 90* V8/V6 (383,350,305,327,283,4.3,3.8,etc) to VW trans.  I am using mine on a 901 tranny, at least until that tranny goes out (using a stock 4.3).<P>There really isn't any "rebuilding" of the VW or porsche trans, unless you have the special tools required.  It requires a jig (or an old VW tranny cut and modified to work as a jig) to reassemble the trans.  Basically the only thing a normal mechanic can replace are seals, a few bushings, etc.  If it needs any gears or syncros replaced, it requires special tooling and knowledge.<P>Buy the latest "Hot VWs" magazine (every magazine rack has it, and it beats the hell out of VW trends for off-road stuff).  They have a TON of adds for VW off roading stuff, as well as VW tranny stuff.  There are prices anywhere from "almost too cheap to be true" to "ha...no thanks!" in there.  For the record, I've yet to come across a situation in the VW scene that isn't a "you get what you pay for" operation.  Since there are so many suppliers out there, there's really no way to beat the competition without cutting costs.<P>Do you plan on building a "sandrail" dune buggy..a rail job, or do you plan on building a "dune buggy" dune buggy that is a fiberglass body on a shortened VW chassis(like speed buggy cartoon <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)?  I get the idea you mean a sandrail, which is tough to pick a specific supplier for; there are LOTS of different choices.  Also, if you plan on building a sandrail, subscribe to "Sand Sports"..there will be an insert in the Hot VWs...it's unbeatable for offroad VW information!<P>I am big into the VW scene, and I've researched a ton of stuff with it.  If you need more info or have more questions, just ask...chances are I'll have some direction to point you in or information I've learned along the way.<P>There are a lot of very cool people in the VW circle, and those things can be heavily abused without blinking an eye.  There's also a MONSTROUS aftermarket following for them...basically if you could dream it up it's been done and can be bought off parts on the shelf.  Very very fun and addictive habit, and even very affordable if you're looking for mild performance.  In the sand my bug would go anywhere the jeeps would, but way more fun doing it, because you get to do it FASTER <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My babies:  <A HREF="http://home1.gte.net/jbnash/bryce.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home1.gte.net/jbnash/bryce.html</A> <P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||11-29-2001||02:27 AM||||There are a lot of porsche and foreign car specific yards. Look in your yellow pages. And your NOT going to find a rear engine porsche transaxle for anything near $200 !||||reg||1
Z||000007||Nashco||11-29-2001||02:50 AM||||I got my 901 for 200 bucks, with 97,000 original miles on it, from a 911.<P>They go on ebay all the time, about one or two a week, usually around 100-300 dollar range.  Here's an example of this weeks deals:<BR> <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=598221660" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=598221660</A>  <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=598475728" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=598475728</A> <P>and a recent completed item:<BR> <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=598012346" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=598012346</A> <P>The 901 is so cheap to obtain, I'm considering buying a second one for when mine breaks.  I can swap it out, and maybe even make a jig out of the old tranny so I can rebuild it myself and use what's good from it in the second one...but probably just have a second tranny "in case."<P>The 901 isn't as strong as the 915 (both came in 911s, 915 is a later design), but is still much stronger than the VW bug and bus transmissions.  A 915 tranny is standard for people looking for an affordable porsche swap, but a 930 (turbo car) trans is what the big shots use.  Now THEY are not affordable..lol...more for the trans, cvs, and axles than I'll have into my entire car after the swap probably!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||11-29-2001||08:28 AM||||damn, you can knock me over. I pay that for mirrors or headlites at junk yards. I guess I stand corrected. Too bad a Porsche drivetrain wouldnt work in a Fiero, for a few hundred bucks, Id have a realllllllly fast one.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||11-29-2001||01:18 PM||||haha..<P>We just spent $4500 on a G50 for a late (c2) 911<P>You can't buy a 2.2ltr 911 motor for $1500 , Webers alone are worth $1200.<P>Expensive stuff.<P><BR>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierospeeder||11-29-2001||11:12 PM||||i just bought a chassis today at the junkyard for my beetle project.<P>I got it for 150 bucks but without floorpans and wheels.  Some of the brake parts are there.<P>it comes with the front suspenion/rear and the trans.<P>I have to go back there tomorrow to get the cv joints off.  The trans is all disconnected except the cv.  I needed a torx  bit (i think).  I went to pepboys and grabbed two.  One i will return.  The torx bit is tad scary, when i came back, some of them seemed to rusted to pull out and i hope i can use my vise grips.  I have to figure out how to lock up the trans, it turns when i try to loosen the screws.  There is no flywheel.  <P>I got my front beam home, and it is heavy.  The guy wanted 175 for the chassis, i told him 150, and took my offer.  Its not too bad, rolling chassis usually go for 300 bucks at bug shows.  Some of the beetle yards in calif want 1000 bucks.  Bogus.<P>Now i just need my POR15 and a mig welder.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Nashco||11-30-2001||12:39 AM||||Yeah, those front beams are REALLY heavy.  I plan on putting an aluminum one in mine after I have the engine swap done and the car on the road.  With some modern fabricating, you can make those cars really light.  I'm hoping with the 4.3 and a really light front end I can go just about anywhere offroad and pull wheelies on the street <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000012||fierospeeder||11-30-2001||01:00 AM||||im going to return the two torx bits i bought at pepboys.  I just realized i got about 3 sets of torx bits in my garage.  I just went out there and took a look.  <P>I also realized that the cvjoints are held in by 12pt hex bits.  So im hoping the ones on the car isn't too stripped.  I did get one off by using a torx bit.  <P>I bought about 400-600 dollars worth of lisle tools when trak auto was having 50% off all their lisle tools.  This was last year, and i spent around 200-300 bucks.  I went through my tools, still in the packages, and found a set of 6 12pt hex bits.  UGH.  I got a lot of tools and i dont know what i have.<P><BR>I put that front end in my moms van by myself.  I did struggle a tad.  I asked my mom to help me bring it to the end of the driveway, when i got it home.  I noticed it was heavy for her.  I was joking that my balls were hurting.  Then she yelled at me that i should have someone helping me lift the front end.<P>I saw a superbug at the yard, its missing one side of the floorpan, and the heater channel is pretty bad.  I want to pick the whole thing and rebuild it at my house.  So i can say "mom look, some how i got 3 beetles now"<P>I gave the junkyard guy one of my extra copy of vw mag and he noticed that its really cheap to get parts.  If he wouldn't give me a good deal on the chassis, i was going to show him pricelists.<P>fuel inj kit+turbo, and ill be quicker then a v8 fiero.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Nashco||11-30-2001||01:53 AM||||Yeah, it's a 12 point, but a 6 point will work just as well if you are gentle and the bolts are ok.  I've never had one that I couldn't get out with either a 6 point or a pair of vise-grips.  I eventually got a 12 point, although I don't think it's required.<P>I don't care much for the superbeetle; I'd only get one if I was making as street car.  They are really easy to lower with adjustable struts, but that suspension sucks for offroading.  I also don't care for the curved front windshield and funky interior, but that's just a personal preference.<P>Be careful what you get yourself into with the "fuel injection+turbo" equation.  Don't forget that along with that comes custom pistons, liners, case machining, cam, oil pump, lifters, rocker arms, oil cooler, oil filter, exhaust, ignition, guages, motor mounts...you get the idea.  I planned on building an 1835cc motor with dual webers and 044 heads...until I really started doing some number crunching.  Building a 130 horse (maximum) VW engine was going to cost about 3,500 bucks.  Swapping in the 4.3 with a smidge under 200 horse is probably going to cost me the same amount, with much more potential in the future.  I can build the 4.3 reliably up to about 300 horse NA, which would wear/break the trans quickly.  The 4.3 is pretty heavy, I believe about 120 pounds heavier, but as I said, this is ideal for offroading.<P>To have your VW be faster than a V8 fiero (completely possible)...you better plan on more than just that turbo kit, is what I was getting to.  You're looking at about 10k dollars, with much more frequent maintenance costs than a similar caliber Fiero.  Also, the Fiero will hook up just as hard as a VW, but handles much better.<P>I think a great motor swap for VWs is the Mazda Rotary and the Ford 2.3 Turbo (Merkur) motor.  I'd have done one of those had I not got such a great deal on my two parts motors and my adapter kit(total under 300 bucks so far).<P>Just food for thought...I love talking VWs <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000014||fierospeeder||11-30-2001||04:17 AM||||the beetles i have right now are going to be road cars.  I have no use for anything offroad at this time.  <P>I did see a kewl beetle 71/72 that was a conversion of a dunebuggy, different apron in the front and no engine lid in the back.  With mini monstertruck tires.  Only thing was the mechanic had to get a lein on the title to sell it.  It was a great price but i never got into doing all the paperwork.  Plus the car hasn't been running for 2 years.  If i get a place for storage, i would buy it.  Thats if he still has it on his property.<P>My mom almost bought me a superbeetle for 3500 bucks.  Perfect condition.  It was a lot of money for her.  We dropped out of the deal and the guy kind of got pissed off.  We were going to bring it to a mechanic before we would buy it and he knew that.  This was about 4 years ago, and i know a year after he still was trying to sell it.<P>The superbeetle is cool in some ways.  I find all the little gadgets in the cars neat.  The super has a diffent dash.  I like the two switch fan and the bugs came with them also.  The odd shaped hood, the luggage compartment having a different look, and the struts.  Ive read that they have a common prob with handeling when the parts wear out.<P><BR>My first car, 74 beetle, didn't run and when i got it home i noticed how bad the condition was with the body.  The rust in illinois on the beetles are horrible.  So its just been a project for a few years now.  Im hoping i can put a car together this summer.  I got most of the tools now, but not all.  And i got a place to work on it now.   Im going to use the chassis i got today, and start with that.  Its a long project for me cause i havn't won the lotto.  But its very addictive.||||reg||1
